  "final": {
    "evidence_sufficiency": "SUFFICIENT",
    "confidence": 88,
    "conclusion": "The evidence supports a real Site A speed advantage, but not the claim that the new scanner is its primary or sole driver. Measurement checks were reliable within sites, although the reproducible cross-site timestamp offset should be corrected when estimating the exact gap. Randomized crossover evidence shows that scanner use improves individual picking time modestly—about 4% (95% CI: 1%–7%)—so it is a contributing factor. However, Site A remained about 5% faster during scanner outages, rejecting the explanation that scanner availability alone produces the full site difference. The persistent 5%–9% advantage after stratification also argues against order size, product mix, shift, or staffing pressure fully explaining the gap. These operating conditions should still be retained as modifiers because the advantage was larger for high-complexity orders. Overall, retain a multifactor explanation involving a modest scanner benefit plus additional Site A-specific process or operational advantages not identified by the opened evidence."
  },
